---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')

const authors = [
	{ name: 'Kang Minkyu', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/' },
]

const links = [
	{ label: 'Project Weasel', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/' },
]

// Qualitative scenario clips (lossless renders compressed for the web).
const clip = (name) => `${base}/clips/${name}.mp4`

// Paper figures (exported from the design/architecture drawings).
const fig = (name) => `${base}/figures/${name}.png`

// System / design figures shown at the back of the page, with very short captions.
const sysFigures = [
	{ f: 'slack', l: 'Slack bridge: the in-game screen floods with text, so human↔agent dialogue moves to Slack — outside the game view.' },
	{ f: 'grouping', l: 'Task groups: agents split into groups (Miner, Farming…); a human can own a group, dividing work across people and agents.' },
	{ f: 'dynamics', l: 'Free communication: agents are not bound to fixed pairs — any agent talks to any other (many-to-many), forming plans dynamically.' },
	{ f: 'observability', l: 'Observability: in-game observation is partial; the text channel is full — yet a model does not reliably know its own state.' },
	{ f: 'update_loop', l: 'Per-agent loop: timestamped Memory → Reflection (insights) → reusable Skills.' },
]

// Scenario groups, ordered as a story: what works → coordination →
// human-in-the-loop → and why the framework is needed (failure cases).
const works = [
	{ src: 'different_tasks', label: 'Eight agents split into different jobs and work in parallel.' },
	{ src: 'log_gathering_a', label: 'Chopping logs to stock shared materials.' },
	{ src: 'log_gathering_b', label: 'Gathering keeps the supply chain moving.' },
]
const failures = [
	{ src: 'farming_stuck_farm', label: 'Without the framework: the farm task stalls and never completes.' },
	{ src: 'farming_stuck_resource', label: 'Without the framework: the agent cannot recover from a missing resource.' },
]
---

<Layout
	title='MineMate: Multi-Agent Emergent Capability with Humans'
	pageTitle='MineMate'
	description='Sub-project of Project Weasel — emergent multi-agent capability with humans in the loop, in the open-ended world of Minecraft.'
	authors={authors}
	links={links}
	heroVideo={clip('open_ended')}
	heroAlt='An open-ended Minecraft world'
	faviconHref={`${base}/minemate.png`}
>
	<h1>Overview</h1>
	<ul>
		<li><b>MineMate</b> is a sub-project of <a href='https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/'>Project Weasel</a>: an <b>8-agent Minecraft village</b> where LLM agents — and a human — cooperate to build, farm, and gather together.</li>
		<li>We study whether useful capability can <b>emerge</b> from many language-model agents acting in a shared world, and how a coordination framework plus a human-in-the-loop make that emergence reliable.</li>
	</ul>

	<h1>Why Minecraft?</h1>
	<p>
		Minecraft is an <b>open-ended, partially-observable sandbox</b>: there is no fixed objective, each
		agent only sees its local surroundings, and goals must be discovered rather than scripted. That makes
		it a strong testbed for <b>multi-agent emergence</b> — capability has to arise from the agents'
		interaction with the world and with each other, not from hand-written rules. The clip above is the
		raw world the agents wake up into.
	</p>

	<h1>Partial observation matters</h1>
	<p>
		An embodied agent only ever sees its own <b>first-person view</b>. From that egocentric vantage
		(left) it often <b>cannot tell how much it has actually accomplished</b>, or what is right beside
		it. The <b>same moment in a third-person 3D view</b> (right) tells a different story — the agent
		has in fact done a great deal of work. Because the agent is constrained to this <b>partial, 3D
		first-person observation</b> of the world, it cannot always judge its own situation correctly: a
		core difficulty of acting under <b>partial observability</b>.
		<span class='muted'>Click either video to enlarge.</span>
	</p>
	<div class='compare'>
		<figure class='cmpcard'>
			<video src={clip('partial_observation')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
			<figcaption>First-person view — all the agent actually sees.</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure class='cmpcard'>
			<video src={clip('partial_observation_3d')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
			<figcaption>Third-person 3D view — the work the agent really did.</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<h1>What the agents do well</h1>
	<p>
		Given a concrete sub-task, each agent performs reliably. The eight agents naturally <b>divide labor</b>
		— logging, mining, and crafting tools — and run those jobs in parallel without colliding.
	</p>
	<div class='clip-grid'>
		{works.map((c) => (
			<figure class='clip'>
				<video src={clip(c.src)} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
				<figcaption>{c.label}</figcaption>
			</figure>
		))}
	</div>

	<h1>Agents coordinate to share resources</h1>
	<p>
		Capability is not just per-agent — it is <b>social</b>. Agents communicate over shared channels to
		request and hand off items. Here an agent needs a crafting table; it asks <b>Nova</b>, and Nova
		brings one over. The exchange emerges from their dialogue, not from a hard-coded trade.
	</p>
	<figure class='clip clip-wide'>
		<video src={clip('nova_communication')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
		<figcaption>An agent asks Nova for a crafting table — and Nova delivers it.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>Humans and agents team up</h1>
	<p>
		With a <b>human in the loop</b>, the agents take direction and contribute to larger projects. This
		building session shows that human–agent teaming is workable: the human sets intent, and the agents
		help realize it together.
	</p>
	<figure class='clip clip-wide'>
		<video src={clip('human_agent_building')} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
		<figcaption>Human and agents building together.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>Why the framework matters</h1>
	<p>
		Our contribution is the <b>coordination framework</b>. Remove it and the <i>same</i> model gets
		<b>stuck</b>: it talks instead of acting, loops on a sub-goal, or cannot recover from a missing
		resource. The two farming attempts below fail exactly where the framework would have kept the team
		unblocked — motivating why the structure is needed.
	</p>
	<div class='clip-grid'>
		{failures.map((c) => (
			<figure class='clip'>
				<video src={clip(c.src)} loop muted playsinline preload='none'></video>
				<figcaption>{c.label}</figcaption>
			</figure>
		))}
	</div>

	<hr />
	<h1>Design space &amp; system</h1>
	<p>
		Beyond the demos, MineMate is studied as an <b>HCI problem</b>: how one human
		coordinates with <i>many</i> agents at once. Single-agent capability is already strong —
		the hard part is <b>teaming</b>. Below is the design space we explore and the system
		pieces that make many-agent control workable. <span class='muted'>Click any figure to enlarge.</span>
	</p>

	<figure class='fig-wide'>
		<img loading='lazy' src={fig('design_space')} alt='MineMate design space' />
		<figcaption>
			<b>Design space.</b> We partition human–agent teaming along three axes:
			<b>model communication</b> (coupled → decoupled → isolated → unstructured group
			structures), <b>observability</b> (partial in-game vs. full in text), and
			<b>user–model interaction</b> (direct, hierarchical, decentralized talking systems).
			Each cell is measured for efficiency and interaction preference.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<div class='figgrid'>
		{sysFigures.map((g) => (
			<figure class='figcard'>
				<img loading='lazy' src={fig(g.f)} alt={g.l} />
				<figcaption>{g.l}</figcaption>
			</figure>
		))}
	</div>

	<h1>Experiment design</h1>
	<p>
		We run a controlled study over a <b>20 × 5 grid</b>. <b>Phase 1 — team composition:</b>
		humans <i>H</i> ∈ {'{'}1, 2, 4, 8{'}'} crossed with agents <i>A</i> ∈ {'{'}1, 2, 4, 8, 16{'}'},
		measuring the marginal value of adding an agent vs. a human across scales.
		<b>Phase 2 — spatial role assignment:</b> humans and agents are placed in a bounded
		<b>House</b> (indoor, repetitive subtasks) or an unbounded <b>Wild</b> (open exploration),
		plus a composite <b>Village</b>. The hypothesis: <i>VLM agents excel at bounded, repetitive
		tasks but degrade in open-ended exploration</i> — so role-aligning agents to bounded work
		should help most. Metrics: task-completion time, throughput, and human workload.
	</p>

	<hr />
	<h1>Acknowledgement</h1>
	<p>
		Part of <a href='https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/'>Project Weasel</a>, maintained by{' '}
		<a href='https://mk040412.github.io/'>Kang Minkyu</a>. Built on the mindcraft agent framework and
		PaperMC, with inference served from Google Vertex AI.
	</p>

	<div id='lightbox' class='lightbox' role='dialog' aria-label='enlarged clip'>
		<img id='lightbox-img' src='' alt='' />
		<video id='lightbox-vid' loop muted playsinline controls></video>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.clip-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
		gap: 16px;
		margin: 16px 0 8px;
	}
	.clip {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.clip video {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 6px;
		background: #000;
	}
	.clip figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 14px;
		margin-top: 6px;
		line-height: 1.4;
	}
	.clip-wide {
		max-width: 800px;
		margin: 16px auto 8px;
	}

	/* Side-by-side comparison (first-person vs 3D view) */
	.compare {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 16px;
		margin: 16px 0 8px;
	}
	.cmpcard {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.cmpcard video {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 6px;
		background: #000;
	}
	.cmpcard figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 14px;
		margin-top: 6px;
		line-height: 1.4;
		text-align: center;
	}

	/* Click any clip to enlarge */
	.clip video,
	.cmpcard video {
		cursor: zoom-in;
		transition: transform 0.12s ease;
	}
	.clip video:hover,
	.cmpcard video:hover {
		transform: scale(1.02);
	}

	/* Click-to-enlarge lightbox */
	.lightbox {
		position: fixed;
		inset: 0;
		background: rgba(10, 16, 28, 0.88);
		display: none;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		z-index: 1000;
		cursor: zoom-out;
		padding: 24px;
	}
	.lightbox.open {
		display: flex;
	}
	.lightbox video,
	.lightbox img {
		max-width: 92vw;
		max-height: 92vh;
		width: auto;
		height: auto;
		border-radius: 10px;
		box-shadow: 0 10px 60px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
	}
	.lightbox img {
		background: #fff;
	}
	#lightbox-img {
		display: none;
	}

	/* Design-space + system figures */
	.fig-wide {
		margin: 18px 0 8px;
	}
	.fig-wide img {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 8px;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		background: #fff;
	}
	.fig-wide figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 14px;
		margin-top: 8px;
		line-height: 1.5;
	}
	.figgrid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
		gap: 16px;
		margin: 16px 0 8px;
		align-items: start;
	}
	.figcard {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.figcard img {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
		border-radius: 8px;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		background: #fff;
	}
	.figcard figcaption {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 13px;
		margin-top: 6px;
		line-height: 1.4;
	}
	/* Click any figure to enlarge */
	.fig-wide img,
	.figcard img {
		cursor: zoom-in;
		transition: transform 0.12s ease;
	}
	.figcard img:hover {
		transform: scale(1.02);
	}

	@media (max-width: 700px) {
		.compare {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>

<script>
	const lb = document.getElementById('lightbox')
	const lbVid = document.getElementById('lightbox-vid') as HTMLVideoElement | null
	const lbImg = document.getElementById('lightbox-img') as HTMLImageElement | null
	if (lb && lbVid && lbImg) {
		const openVid = (src: string) => {
			lbImg.style.display = 'none'
			lbImg.removeAttribute('src')
			lbVid.style.display = 'block'
			lbVid.setAttribute('src', src)
			lb.classList.add('open')
			const p = lbVid.play()
			if (p && p.catch) p.catch(() => {})
		}
		const openImg = (src: string, alt: string) => {
			lbVid.pause()
			lbVid.removeAttribute('src')
			lbVid.style.display = 'none'
			lbImg.style.display = 'block'
			lbImg.setAttribute('src', src)
			lbImg.setAttribute('alt', alt)
			lb.classList.add('open')
		}
		const close = () => {
			lb.classList.remove('open')
			lbVid.pause()
			lbVid.removeAttribute('src')
			lbImg.removeAttribute('src')
		}
		document.querySelectorAll('.clip video, .cmpcard video').forEach((v) => {
			v.addEventListener('click', () => openVid(v.getAttribute('src') || ''))
		})
		document.querySelectorAll('.fig-wide img, .figcard img').forEach((im) => {
			im.addEventListener('click', () => openImg(im.getAttribute('src') || '', im.getAttribute('alt') || ''))
		})
		lb.addEventListener('click', close)
		document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
			if (e.key === 'Escape') close()
		})
	}

	// Play only the videos near/in the viewport so the browser never decodes
	// ~10 clips at once (that simultaneous decoding is what makes playback stutter).
	const vids = document.querySelectorAll('.clip video, .cmpcard video')
	if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
		const io = new IntersectionObserver(
			(entries) => {
				entries.forEach((e) => {
					const v = e.target as HTMLVideoElement
					if (e.isIntersecting) {
						if (v.preload !== 'auto') v.preload = 'auto'
						const p = v.play()
						if (p && p.catch) p.catch(() => {})
					} else {
						v.pause()
					}
				})
			},
			{ rootMargin: '200px 0px', threshold: 0.25 }
		)
		vids.forEach((v) => io.observe(v))
	} else {
		// Fallback: no IntersectionObserver — just autoplay everything.
		vids.forEach((v) => {
			const el = v as HTMLVideoElement
			el.preload = 'auto'
			const p = el.play()
			if (p && p.catch) p.catch(() => {})
		})
	}
</script>
